Below is a step‐by‐step decomposition of the mission description into a hierarchical goal model for a multi‐robot system. In this design, we break the overall mission into two primary phases—a collection phase and a delivery phase—with additional provisions for battery‐level fallback and parallel execution when multiple resources are required. Each goal and task is justified by the mission’s requirements.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 1. Overall Mission Goal  
─────────────────────────────

We begin by defining the top‐level goal that the robot must achieve. Since the mission has the target state “all required resources delivered to the requesting agent,” we classify it as an Achieve Goal.

• G1: Mission Execution: Collect & Deliver Resources  
 – Text: “Execute mission to collect required resources from storage and deliver them to the requesting agent.”  
 – Runtime: Sequential (“;”)  
 – Goal Type: Achieve  
 – Target Condition: All required resources delivered to requesting agent.  
 – Relation: AND (G2 and G3)  
 – Justification: The mission must complete both the collection and delivery phases in sequence, and if multiple items are needed, each collect‐deliver pair must complete successfully.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 2. Collection Phase Decomposition  
─────────────────────────────

The collection phase requires the robot to navigate to a storage, request the resource, and wait until it is retrieved. However, if the battery level falls below 10%, a fallback is activated so that the robot returns to the recharging station and hands off the mission.

• G2: Collection Phase  
 – Text: “Navigate to storage, request resource, and wait for retrieval.”  
 – Runtime: FALLBACK(AT5, AT1; AT2; AT3)  
  • Here, the normal branch is a sequential execution (AT1; AT2; AT3) and the fallback alternative is AT5.  
 – Goal Type: Perform  
 – Justification: The collection phase consists of sequential tasks (navigation, resource request, waiting) with an alternative (return to recharging) triggered when the battery is too low.

Within G2, the associated tasks are:

• AT1: Navigate to Storage  
 – Text: “Navigate to the designated storage location to collect the resource.”  
 – Relation: Sequential (as part of G2)  
 – Location: Storage Location  
 – Number of Robots: [1]  
 – Justification: A single robot performs the navigation task.

• AT2: Request Resource  
 – Text: “Send a message to the storage with the precise specification of the requested resource.”  
 – Relation: Sequential (as part of G2)  
 – Location: Storage  
 – Number of Robots: [1]  
 – Justification: A single robot handles the communication with the storage.

• AT3: Wait for Resource Retrieval  
 – Text: “Wait at the storage until the resource is retrieved.”  
 – Relation: Sequential (as part of G2)  
 – Location: Storage  
 – Number of Robots: [1]  
 – Justification: The robot must remain at the storage until the resource is available.

• AT5: Return to Recharging Station (Collection Fallback)  
 – Text: “Navigate back to the recharging station when battery is low during collection.”  
 – Relation: Fallback alternative in G2  
 – Location: Recharging Station  
 – Number of Robots: [1]  
 – Justification: Triggered if the battery level falls below 10% during collection, ensuring the robot can recharge and the mission is safely handed off.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 3. Delivery Phase Decomposition  
─────────────────────────────

The delivery phase is more complex because it involves repeated runs to deliver the resource. In addition, if the battery level falls below 30%, the robot must return the resource to a checkpoint and assign the remaining task to another robot. If that fallback also fails, an alert is triggered and a report is sent to the sector manager.

We represent this with nested fallbacks. Under normal conditions, the robot performs the delivery (AT6) and a battery check (AT7). If the battery check fails (indicating low battery), the robot falls back to returning the resource to a checkpoint (AT8); if that also fails, it triggers an alert (AT9).

• G3: Delivery Phase  
 – Text: “Deliver resources to the specified location, perform battery check, and handle fallback if battery is low.”  
 – Runtime: FALLBACK(AT9, FALLBACK((AT6; AT7), AT8))  
  • Interpretation:  
   – Inner Fallback: FALLBACK((AT6; AT7), AT8)  
    ◦ Primary branch: normal delivery (AT6 followed by AT7)  
    ◦ Fallback alternative: return resource to checkpoint (AT8)  
   – Outer Fallback: If AT8 fails, then execute AT9 (trigger alert)  
 – Goal Type: Perform  
 – Justification: The delivery phase must execute normal delivery when conditions allow, but safely handle low battery situations with appropriate fallbacks.

Within G3, the associated tasks are:

• AT6: Deliver Resource  
 – Text: “Transport the collected resource to the specified delivery location.”  
 – Relation: Primary branch in G3  
 – Location: Specified Delivery Location  
 – Number of Robots: [1]  
 – Justification: Under normal conditions, a single robot delivers the resource.

• AT7: Battery Check in Delivery Phase  
 – Text: “Check battery level during delivery; proceed if sufficient, otherwise trigger fallback.”  
 – Relation: Sequential (part of G3 primary branch)  
 – Location: Along Delivery Route  
 – Number of Robots: [1]  
 – Justification: Ensures the robot’s battery is adequate for completing delivery.

• AT8: Return Resource to Checkpoint (Delivery Fallback)  
 – Text: “Return the resource to a checkpoint if battery is low during delivery.”  
 – Relation: Fallback alternative in G3  
 – Location: Checkpoint  
 – Number of Robots: [1]  
 – Justification: Activated when the battery check (AT7) indicates low battery; ensures the resource is secured.

• AT9: Trigger Alert and Send Report  
 – Text: “Trigger an alert and send a report to the sector manager if the resource cannot be returned to a checkpoint.”  
 – Relation: Fallback alternative in G3  
 – Location: Sector Manager’s Office / Control Center  
 – Number of Robots: [1]  
 – Justification: Serves as a final safety measure if the checkpoint return fails.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 4. Parallel Execution Option  
─────────────────────────────

When multiple items are required from different storages, the mission allows for the assignment of multiple robots to execute collect‐deliver tasks concurrently. We represent this as an additional goal that uses parallel execution.

• G4: Parallel Execution of Collect-Deliver Tasks  
 – Text: “Execute multiple collect‐deliver tasks concurrently for different storage locations.”  
 – Runtime: “#” (parallel)  
 – Goal Type: Perform  
 – Justification: Independent collect‐deliver tasks can run in parallel to reduce overall mission time.

